gm-method/rendering (tinting the SUBMERGED part of arbitrary three.js scene objects when the water look lives in a PRE-composited external pass: patch THREE.ShaderChunk fog chunks globally, never per-material): when a terrain/water engine composites its water color to the canvas BEFORE the three scene renders over it (autoClear=false), scene objects' below-waterline fragments depth-win and overwrite the water untinted -- no fix on the engine side can reach them. The one choke point that covers EVERY material tier (GLTF MeshStandardMaterial, batched/instanced Lambert tiers created LAZILY inside an SDK, primitives, and VRM MToon whose fragment also includes fog_fragment) is a one-time THREE.ShaderChunk patch on the fog chunks, applied BEFORE any program compiles: append a varying to fog_pars_vertex/fog_vertex, a const + tint branch to fog_pars_fragment/fog_fragment, placed OUTSIDE the #ifdef USE_FOG guards so even fog:false materials get it. Recover world-Y without a per-vertex mat4 inverse: viewMatrix is rigid, so worldY = dot(vec3(viewMatrix[0][1],viewMatrix[1][1],viewMatrix[2][1]), mvPosition.xyz) + cameraPosition.y -- and because mvPosition already includes instanceMatrix and skinning, the result is instancing/skinning-correct for free. For a per-world constant (like a sea level fixed at frame creation), splice a GLSL const literal into the chunk and mark already-compiled materials needsUpdate (one-time recompile during loading); lazily-compiled materials pick the literal up automatically. Rejected alternatives and why: per-material onBeforeCompile misses SDK-lazy materials; global clipping-plane two-pass doubles the whole scene render; screen-space post needs a samplable depth the default framebuffer doesn't give; THREE.Fog is camera-radial and cannot express a horizontal plane.